%N A400612 a(n) is the number of divisors d of n such that the last digit of d is equal to the first digit of n.
%C A400612 Inspired by A330348, A357299, and the analog A399629.
%C A400612 Some properties:
%C A400612 1) For primes p:
%C A400612    if p has only one digit, then a(2) = a(3) = a(5) = a(7) = 1 since p | p.
%C A400612    if p has at least two digits, there are three possibilities:
%C A400612      a(p) = 0 iff p does not start with 1 (examples: 23, 29, 31, 37, ...).
%C A400612      a(p) = 1 iff p starts with 1 and not ends with 1, since only 1 | p.
%C A400612      a(p) = 2 iff p starts and ends with 1, since 1 | p and p | p <=> p in A062332.
%C A400612 2) For repdigits:
%C A400612    if m is a one-digit repdigit, then a(m) = 1 since m | m.
%C A400612    if m is a repdigit with at least two digits, say m = kk...k, then a(m) >= 2, since both k | m and m | m and both end in k.
%C A400612 3) For numbers starting with some particular digit:
%C A400612    if m starts with digit d and d | n, then a(m) >= 1, some examples:
%C A400612      if m starts with 1: a(m) >= 1 since 1 | m.
%C A400612      if m even starts with 2: a(m) >= 1.
%C A400612      if m starts with 5 and ends with 0 or 5, then a(m) >= 1.
%C A400612    if m is odd and starts with an even digit, then a(m) = 0.
%C A400612 The maximal length of a string of consecutive zeros is 8. The first such occurrence is a(9181) = a(9182) = ... = a(9188) = 0; further occurrences begin at 91360 and 93601: a(91360) = ... = a(91367) = 0, a(93601) = ... = a(93608) = 0. In particular, a string of 8 consecutive zeros can occur only for numbers starting with 9.
%e A400612 39 has 4 divisors: {1, 3, 13, 39}. Of these, two end with the first digit of 39, namely 3 and 13. Hence a(39) = 2.
%t A400612 a[n_] := DivisorSum[n, 1 &, IntegerDigits[n][[1]] == Mod[#, 10] &]; Array[a, 94] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Oct 01 2026 *)
%o A400612 (Python)
%o A400612 from sympy import divisors
%o A400612 def a(n): f = int(str(n)[0]); return sum(1 for d in divisors(n, generator=True) if d%10 == f)
%o A400612 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 95)]) # _Michael S. Branicky_, Oct 01 2026
%Y A400612 Cf. A062332, A400613.
%Y A400612 Cf. A330348 (last digit), A357299 (first digit), A399629 (analog).
